content.text:
The automatic reply on your second comment, about letting people see all settings, describes OrcaSlicer's settings structure wrongly. It says settings form one chain, Printer > Filament(s) > Process > Plate > Object, with each level overriding the one before it. OrcaSlicer doesn't work that way. I checked against the upstream OrcaSlicer code:

- **Three separate presets.** Printer, filament and process are independent. Process settings don't override filament settings, and filament settings don't override printer settings in general.
- **Filaments override only a few printer settings.** The filament preset's "setting overrides" cover retraction, z-hop, wipe and similar. Each filament slot has its own filament preset.
- **Plates override only a few settings.** A plate's own settings are bed type, print sequence, first-layer filament order and spiral vase. They're not a full settings layer.
- **Objects override process settings.** Objects, their parts, modifiers and height ranges can each override process settings.

Some of the reply's ideas still fit:
- keep the setup card for changes only, and open the full list from a "View all settings" link;
- show each setting's final value for whatever is selected;
- group settings by task, with a badge saying where each value comes from;
- add search and an "only changed" filter;
- for multiple filaments, compare the slots side by side.

But any mockup should follow the real structure above. For example, a source badge would read "Process", "PLA · slot 2" or "Object: Bracket", and there would be no plate layer for most settings.

I haven't changed anything in the canvas, and I've left both threads open because each ends on a question for you:
- **Left-edge stripe on the setup cards:** keep it as it is, or remove it?
- **All-settings view:** should I mock it as new boards next to the setup card, built on the real structure above? I'd draw three:
  - the panel showing the whole project;
  - the same panel with one object selected;
  - a side-by-side comparison of several filaments.

